.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 
This page documents libusb's synchronous (blocking) API for USB device I/O\&. 

This interface is easy to use but has some limitations\&. More advanced users may wish to consider using the \fBasynchronous I/O API\fP instead\&. 
.SH "Function Documentation"
.PP 
.SS "int libusb_control_transfer (\fBlibusb_device_handle\fP * dev_handle, uint8_t bmRequestType, uint8_t bRequest, uint16_t wValue, uint16_t wIndex, unsigned char * data, uint16_t wLength, unsigned int timeout)"

.PP
Perform a USB control transfer\&. The direction of the transfer is inferred from the bmRequestType field of the setup packet\&.

.PP
The wValue, wIndex and wLength fields values should be given in host-endian byte order\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIdev_handle\fP a handle for the device to communicate with 
.br
\fIbmRequestType\fP the request type field for the setup packet 
.br
\fIbRequest\fP the request field for the setup packet 
.br
\fIwValue\fP the value field for the setup packet 
.br
\fIwIndex\fP the index field for the setup packet 
.br
\fIdata\fP a suitably-sized data buffer for either input or output (depending on direction bits within bmRequestType) 
.br
\fIwLength\fP the length field for the setup packet\&. The data buffer should be at least this size\&. 
.br
\fItimeout\fP timeout (in milliseconds) that this function should wait before giving up due to no response being received\&. For an unlimited timeout, use value 0\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
on success, the number of bytes actually transferred 

.PP
\fBLIBUSB_ERROR_TIMEOUT\fP if the transfer timed out 

.PP
\fBLIBUSB_ERROR_PIPE\fP if the control request was not supported by the device 

.PP
\fBLIBUSB_ERROR_NO_DEVICE\fP if the device has been disconnected 

.PP
\fBLIBUSB_ERROR_BUSY\fP if called from event handling context 

.PP
\fBLIBUSB_ERROR_INVALID_PARAM\fP if the transfer size is larger than the operating system and/or hardware can support (see \fBTransfer length limitations\fP) 

.PP
another LIBUSB_ERROR code on other failures 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int libusb_bulk_transfer (\fBlibusb_device_handle\fP * dev_handle, unsigned char endpoint, unsigned char * data, int length, int * transferred, unsigned int timeout)"

.PP
Perform a USB bulk transfer\&. The direction of the transfer is inferred from the direction bits of the endpoint address\&.

.PP
For bulk reads, the \fRlength\fP field indicates the maximum length of data you are expecting to receive\&. If less data arrives than expected, this function will return that data, so be sure to check the \fRtransferred\fP output parameter\&.

.PP
You should also check the \fRtransferred\fP parameter for bulk writes\&. Not all of the data may have been written\&.

.PP
Also check \fRtransferred\fP when dealing with a timeout error code\&. libusb may have to split your transfer into a number of chunks to satisfy underlying O/S requirements, meaning that the timeout may expire after the first few chunks have completed\&. libusb is careful not to lose any data that may have been transferred; do not assume that timeout conditions indicate a complete lack of I/O\&. See \fBTimeouts\fP for more details\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIdev_handle\fP a handle for the device to communicate with 
.br
\fIendpoint\fP the address of a valid endpoint to communicate with 
.br
\fIdata\fP a suitably-sized data buffer for either input or output (depending on endpoint) 
.br
\fIlength\fP for bulk writes, the number of bytes from data to be sent\&. for bulk reads, the maximum number of bytes to receive into the data buffer\&. 
.br
\fItransferred\fP output location for the number of bytes actually transferred\&. Since version 1\&.0\&.21 (\fBLIBUSB_API_VERSION\fP >= 0x01000105), it is legal to pass a NULL pointer if you do not wish to receive this information\&. 
.br
\fItimeout\fP timeout (in milliseconds) that this function should wait before giving up due to no response being received\&. For an unlimited timeout, use value 0\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
0 on success (and populates \fRtransferred\fP) 

.PP
\fBLIBUSB_ERROR_TIMEOUT\fP if the transfer timed out (and populates \fRtransferred\fP) 

.PP
\fBLIBUSB_ERROR_PIPE\fP if the endpoint halted 

.PP
\fBLIBUSB_ERROR_OVERFLOW\fP if the device offered more data, see \fBPackets and overflows\fP 

.PP
\fBLIBUSB_ERROR_NO_DEVICE\fP if the device has been disconnected 

.PP
\fBLIBUSB_ERROR_BUSY\fP if called from event handling context 

.PP
\fBLIBUSB_ERROR_INVALID_PARAM\fP if the transfer size is larger than the operating system and/or hardware can support (see \fBTransfer length limitations\fP) 

.PP
another LIBUSB_ERROR code on other failures 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int libusb_interrupt_transfer (\fBlibusb_device_handle\fP * dev_handle, unsigned char endpoint, unsigned char * data, int length, int * transferred, unsigned int timeout)"

.PP
Perform a USB interrupt transfer\&. The direction of the transfer is inferred from the direction bits of the endpoint address\&.

.PP
For interrupt reads, the \fRlength\fP field indicates the maximum length of data you are expecting to receive\&. If less data arrives than expected, this function will return that data, so be sure to check the \fRtransferred\fP output parameter\&.

.PP
You should also check the \fRtransferred\fP parameter for interrupt writes\&. Not all of the data may have been written\&.

.PP
Also check \fRtransferred\fP when dealing with a timeout error code\&. libusb may have to split your transfer into a number of chunks to satisfy underlying O/S requirements, meaning that the timeout may expire after the first few chunks have completed\&. libusb is careful not to lose any data that may have been transferred; do not assume that timeout conditions indicate a complete lack of I/O\&. See \fBTimeouts\fP for more details\&.

.PP
The default endpoint bInterval value is used as the polling interval\&.

.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIdev_handle\fP a handle for the device to communicate with 
.br
\fIendpoint\fP the address of a valid endpoint to communicate with 
.br
\fIdata\fP a suitably-sized data buffer for either input or output (depending on endpoint) 
.br
\fIlength\fP for bulk writes, the number of bytes from data to be sent\&. for bulk reads, the maximum number of bytes to receive into the data buffer\&. 
.br
\fItransferred\fP output location for the number of bytes actually transferred\&. Will never be negative\&. Since version 1\&.0\&.21 (\fBLIBUSB_API_VERSION\fP >= 0x01000105), it is legal to pass a NULL pointer if you do not wish to receive this information\&. 
.br
\fItimeout\fP timeout (in milliseconds) that this function should wait before giving up due to no response being received\&. For an unlimited timeout, use value 0\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
0 on success (and populates \fRtransferred\fP) 

.PP
\fBLIBUSB_ERROR_TIMEOUT\fP if the transfer timed out 

.PP
\fBLIBUSB_ERROR_PIPE\fP if the endpoint halted 

.PP
\fBLIBUSB_ERROR_OVERFLOW\fP if the device offered more data, see \fBPackets and overflows\fP 

.PP
\fBLIBUSB_ERROR_NO_DEVICE\fP if the device has been disconnected 

.PP
\fBLIBUSB_ERROR_BUSY\fP if called from event handling context 

.PP
\fBLIBUSB_ERROR_INVALID_PARAM\fP if the transfer size is larger than the operating system and/or hardware can support (see \fBTransfer length limitations\fP) 

.PP
another LIBUSB_ERROR code on other error 
.RE
.PP

